ss the universe only really have the  power they have  because people believe in them and  because people worship them the  antithesis to this  is that if people stop believing in gods  and stop worshipping gods then in turn  gods will lose their power and that was  really where the crux of of gore the god  butcher's  role came into play in the sense that  his desire was not to kill innocent  believers right that was never really  his goal his vengeance was against the  gods now he did believe in collateral  damage right so if some believers run up  on him trying to kill him because he's  trying to kill one of their gods well  then you know sorry about your luck  right but at the end of the day he  wouldn't go out of his way and  annihilate an entire planet of innocent  people for the sheer fun of it right he  wasn't like thanos in that way and so  what had happened was a kind of  manifestation of gore's son agar had  appeared  and then basically shunned his father  and said you have become everything that  you hate  and then in turn started praying to thor  this alongside all the other gods that  it had basically survived from across  all time in space  praying to thor basically boosted his  power and it ultimately led to thor  killing gore but following that it was  kind of a funny thing gore you talk to  anybody who read  jason aaron's god of thunder and they'll  tell you gore was definitively one of  the greatest comic book villains ever  created  literally across the board right just in  terms of interest his intrigue  why he did what he did his motivations  how he was written he was just one of  the most compelling villains that marvel  had ever created and people wanted to  see him come back  right they wanted to see him return and  so ultimately he did  the biggest problem that you had with  this and i don't know if i'd necessarily  call it a problem honestly the people  who complained about it it was kind of  smatterings across the internet wasn't  really a great big huge  beef with it but the entirety of gore's  return and the motivation and really  what he did  seemed to kind of betray the motivation  of his character initially right in the  sense that al gore wanted to do was kill  gods with gore's return  a couple things had happened since then  the first was that with loki having  eradicated all life on earth  that that life had basically been in the  process of returning  due to the power of all father thor it  was always all the way to the point  where you actually had  citizens on earth right people on earth  who were living their lives nowhere near  advanced as  modern society it was pretty advanced  right so let's say humanity  20 000 years ago but that's basically  what you saw living in the world people  who kind of believed in thor and all  that kind of good stuff  the other part of this is that loki in  this far-flung future had managed to get  his hands on the all-black necro sword  and the goal was to use it to basically  kill all father thor  the biggest issue with this is that  gore's consciousness had existed within  the sword  and so when loki had seized it he had  also resurrected war  gorg basically impaled loki seemingly  killing him and then in turn went to go  fight all father thor so  unlike the original gore the god butcher  story you didn't see thor's from three  different eras facing off against score  instead  it was just all father thor in the  future but where things shifted up  is that the fight moved away from being  what was  really more of like a glorified bare  knuckle battle and in turn  it turned into like this massive  conflict now a lot of this was due to  the fact that all father thor had  possessed such an immense level of power  at that point in time literally the  ability to essentially create life and  then at the same time  gore had to become something equally as  powerful which he'd essentially become  now a lot of that seemed to be  predicated on what was going on with 